{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/219088-on-harvesting-glamour","id":219088,"title":"On Harvesting Glamour","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-08-05T08:16:31-07:00","id":219088,"title":"On Harvesting Glamour","content":"So, is it me, or does Harvesting Glamour feel very &quot;meh?&quot; It doesn't -seem- to really add anything to the game or play into the Changeling's themes, and its so trivially gotten that its barely a real limit. In play, that I have noticed, players tend to notice someone being emotional and go &quot;P.S. Glamour plx,&quot; and only rarely actually go out and do things specifically to harvest glamour. I have a bit of an issue with this because &quot;notice people are being emotional,&quot; while rewarded, adds nothing to the player's experiences. In fact, I'd argue that it takes an emotional or drama filled moment and pulls them out of the story as they bring sort of a mechanical awareness to whats going on. Even when they do go out and harvest Glamour, it just doesn't feel particularly interesting--a speed bump to get back to the story. In the group I'm STing for now, the players tend to forget to Harvest completely, except when they actually use their powers and realize they are short. Then there's a scramble of, &quot;Uh, is -this- emotional enough to...?&quot;<br /> <br /> Are my experiences just me, or is this something other people have experienced? Is there something I'm missing? Are there any suggestions on how to make Harvesting Glamour more evocative? I considered centering it around collecting and consuming intangibles, but that may feel too Demon-y. I also considered basing it off of resolving conditions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-08-05T16:31:48-07:00","id":219602,"title":"","content":"Hey, I'm still new here and all, but I have a little experience with Changeling gameplay. What my friends and I usually do with the Harvest is that the emotion needs to be triggered or enhanced by some action of the changeling. He needs to interact with the source in order to actually get any Glamour out of it.<br /> <br /> One of my character is a DJ and she harvests during her shows. Since she's responsible for the music, she's causing their euforia and she gets Glamour from that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2480-may-higurashe","name":"May Higurashe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-08-06T07:18:00-07:00","id":220153,"title":"","content":"I would suggest two things about Harvesting that would make it more 'mythic' and draw more action from Players.<br /> <br /> 1. Harvest creates a relationship. You can harvest strangers, it happens a lot, but you may never see that person again. So you get some basic Harvest per rules. But if you Harvest the same group, the same Homeless Person, or the same group of High Schoolers week in and week out, you learn about them. You anticipate their moods and locations. Like a hunter and quarry, you pay attention and become canny about the prey.<br /> <br /> 2. Harvest creates consequence. You power up. You restore your snap-crackle. Depending on the person you are Harvesting from, it could be a one-night-stand, or it could be something that affects both parties. Your own GM would have to make the call about long term effects. Traditional stories talk about the Fae attraction to the Creativity of mortals. Does Harvesting diminish a person's creative spark? If you Harvest the fear of a troop of boy scouts in the woods, is it harder for those boys to feel fear for a week? Or do they lose sleep? Or do they more easily become afraid the next time something odd happens?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Arref</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000FF\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i>......making magic since 1154 B.C.</i></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2062-arref","name":"Arref","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-08-06T08:42:49-07:00","id":220251,"title":"","content":"I just joined, and while I only have a moderate amount of actual play experience, I've been interested in and reading the WoD rulebooks for a while.<br /> <br /> I know what you mean, with it taking players out of the moment, but this seems to be the case with just about every mana-equivalent across all the games. A lot of the time it's such a purely-mechanical act that it really can't be smoothly done without breaking flow. Even Vampire has this issue sometimes.<br /> <br /> But I think if you want it to be more interesting, you should describe and embellish it a bit more. Harvesting glamour is literally seeing and drinking deep of a mortal's emotions. If done correctly, that can add to the tone of the scene. When a changeling decides to harvest glamour from a grieving widow, the intensity and wealth of the glamour they find is a great opportunity to drive how <i>how</i> grief-stricken this person is.<br /> <br /> And this would work with any emotion. There's a fiction in one of the books that describes how Jack Tallow sees a bar he goes to, where he sees little sprites of emotion flitting about and so forth. Use that opportunity to describe how the characters really see the world and, as I said before, use it to strengthen the impact of the emotional scene. If the players don't seem to care, then it's not really the game's fault... Your players may just not be interested in that aspect of roleplaying.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3655-elliegray","name":"elliegray","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}